  "title": "Paxton Drops Two Abortion Pill Lawsuits. How Far Can Texas Law Reach?",
  "deck": "Attorney General-elect Nathan Johnson says he’ll stop suing out-of-state providers. Texas law still gives eligible private plaintiffs a way to bring cases.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "U.S. Politics",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T17:20:16.377Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-21T17:20:16.377Z",
  "body": [
    "Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton withdrew two lawsuits on Friday against out-of-state providers accused of supplying abortion pills to Texans. His successor, Attorney General-elect Nathan Johnson, has already said he will stop bringing that kind of case. The withdrawals leave a question that matters beyond Texas: When a prescription crosses a state line, how far can Texas go to enforce its law?",
    "## Which cases Texas dropped",
    "Paxton’s office filed notices of nonsuit on Nov. 20 in both cases, The Texas Tribune reported. One targeted Delaware nurse practitioner Debra Lynch, who operates Her Safe Harbor. The other targeted Aid Access and doctors Remy Coeytaux and Rebecca Gomperts. Paxton’s office alleged that the defendants supplied or helped send abortion medication to Texas residents.",
    "The state withdrew its own claims. The notices did not produce a ruling on whether Texas could enforce its restrictions against those defendants. The Tribune reported that Coeytaux also faces a separate suit brought by a Galveston County man. That private case was distinct from the two Paxton withdrew.",
    "## Where state lines matter",
    "Texas House Bill 7 reaches the mailing, delivery and prescribing of abortion-inducing drugs to or from Texas, subject to statutory exceptions. It also permits eligible private plaintiffs to bring civil suits. Other states have adopted shield laws that can limit their officials’ cooperation with out-of-state proceedings over care protected at home.",
    "A separate case in New York shows the practical difference between obtaining a judgment in Texas and enforcing it elsewhere. Texas obtained a civil judgment against New York physician Margaret Carpenter after accusing her of prescribing abortion medication to a Texas patient. An Ulster County clerk refused to file the judgment, citing New York’s shield law. A New York judge dismissed Texas’ challenge to that refusal in October 2025, the Associated Press reported.",
    "## What Nathan Johnson says he’ll do",
    "Johnson told The Texas Tribune after the election that he would stop suing out-of-state doctors who send abortion medication to Texans. That pledge concerns how his office will use its enforcement authority. House Bill 7 remains on the books, including its provisions for private lawsuits.",
    "The next test could therefore come from a private case. If a Texas plaintiff obtains a judgment against a provider elsewhere, the next question is whether officials in the provider’s state will help enforce it."
  ],
